A||||8||ChadMan||Shock/strut replacement||||
Z||000000||ChadMan||09-09-1999||10:02 PM||||I'm going to have to replace the shocks and struts on my 86 SE soon.  I know replacing the front shocks is an easy job, but how tough is replacing the struts?  Will I need a spring compressor?  Any tips?<P>Also, anybody have any recommendations in reasonably priced shocks/struts?  I'm sure whatever I get will be a big improvement - I think the struts are the originals!||||reg||
Z||000001||theogre||09-09-1999||10:22 PM||||the front shocks are no big deal, correct there.......<P>DO NOT attempt to disassemble the struts without the propper tools. Your best bet...... remove the strut assembly and take it to a local shop to have the new strut mated to the spring. The spring work can be Very dangerous, even with the right tools. Most shops only charge a few dollars for this service.<P>The strut assembly is retained by 3(Three) bolts/nuts at the top, and 2 on the bottom. DO NOT remove the nut from the strut ram in the center of the top bearing unit. (Note: one bearing bolt holds the bracket for the vent grates on most models.)<P>You will likely also need new "cam bolts" for the bottom. The new strut base is likely slotted and the new cam bolts are use to adjust wheel alignment. (The original units are not slotted for adjustment.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-09-1999).]||||reg||
Z||000002||mwbackus||09-10-1999||12:07 AM||||Autozone rents strut spring compressors for free, you only need a small refundable deposit.  If common sense is used, I would say that most could manage this job safely.  The bottom strut bolts are IMO, the biggest challenge in this job, since they are usually rusted and horribly seized up (BFH needed).  The top bolts on the front shocks can also be a bugger due to rust.  I found that if you pry the rubber shock eye out from below the large washer, it will allow the washer to slide down enough that one can easily cut the bolt off below the nut with a jigsaw. <P>Mike||||reg||
Z||000003||ChadMan||09-10-1999||09:15 AM||||No rusty bolt is safe from me when I borrow my dad's Sawzall!!!  And what's a BFH?  A big f'ing hammer?  LOL<P>Haynes makes this job sound easy (support lower control arm to keep spring compressed, unbolt 5 bolts, remove old strut), but I trust you guys more.  Do you have to remove the spring from the car in the course of replacing the strut?||||reg||
Z||000004||theogre||09-10-1999||09:34 AM||||you remove the whole unit, spring, top bearing, and all, from the car. You then compress the spring with a tool made for the job. then you remove the nut from the strut top that holds the bearing unit.||||reg||
Z||000005||fastonpumpgas||09-10-1999||12:43 PM||||I dont know about my fiero but on other cars an old trick is to paint the lower part of the strut , that way you can get it back to and not worry about tire wear , but it should still be realigned  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||
Z||000006||mwbackus||09-10-1999||03:47 PM||||Yes, a BFH is exactly as you describe, a large hammer, more precisly a sledge type of considerable weight.  I have found it to be  a valuable tool for many of the challenges encountered while fixing my 10yr old car.  It seems to aid the removal of seized nuts and bolts, when used with a good penetrating oil such as WD-40 and several alcholic beverages). Autozone does not rent the BFH, so you may have to purchase one if needed. :-)<P>Mike||||reg||
Z||000007||batboy||09-11-1999||11:56 AM||||Fast is right. After replacing the rear struts, you are suppose to do a four wheel alignment. Rear tire wear can be pretty bad if the rear alignment is out of whack. Of course, handling is not as good either.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-11-1999||01:30 PM||||the new slotted strut bottoms pretty much guarantee that the alignment will be off.||||reg||7
